\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSHost object holds network name and address information for a host. You use this class to get the current host\'27s name and address and to look up other hosts by name or by address. \
\fs14\
\fs28 To create an NSHost, use the \b currentHost\b0 , \b hostWithAddress:\b0 , or \b hostWithName:\b0  class methods (don\'27t use \b alloc\b0  and \b init\b0 ). These methods use available network administration services (such as NetInfo or the Domain Name Service) to discover all names and addresses for the host requested. They don\'27t attempt to contact the host itself, however. This avoids untimely delays due to a host being unavailable, but it may result in incomplete information about the host.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSHost instance contains all of the network addresses and names discovered for a given host by the network administration services. Each NSHost typically contains one unique address, but it may have more than one name. If an NSHost has more than one name, the additional names are variations on the same name, typically the basic host name plus the fully qualified domain name. For example, with a host name \'aasales\'ba in the domain \'aaanycorp.com\'ba, an NSHost can hold both the names \'aasales\'ba and \'aasales.anycorp.com\'ba.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The NSHost class maintains a cache of previously created instances so that requests for an existing NSHost return that object instead of creating a new one. Use the \b setHostCacheEnabled:\b0  method to turn the cache off, forcing lookup of hosts as they\'27re requested. You can also use the \b flushHostCache\b0  method to clear the cache of its entries so that subsequent requests look up the host information and create new instances.\
\fs14\
